.TH RADIOCHK 1
.SH NAME
radiochk \- check what the radio device refuses
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B radiochk
.RI [ recv ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Radiochk
exercises the two things
.IR radio (3)
refuses, which are the part of its design a shell cannot reach: the
console line discipline will not carry a command line long enough to
make an oversize write, so there is no other way to test them.
.PP
With no argument it checks the write side, which needs no second
station: a write one byte past the payload budget must fail and the
error must name the budget, and a legal write must be accepted.
.PP
.B recv
checks the read side and waits for a packet from somewhere.  It asks for
one byte of it first, which must fail rather than hand back a byte and
discard the rest, and then reads it whole and prints it \- the same
packet, still queued after the read that was refused.
.PP
The last lines are a count and, only if every check ran and none failed,
.BR RADIOCHK-OK .
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/radiochk.b
.SH EXAMPLE
Two emulated boards on one socket, one listening and one sending:
.IP
.EX
qemu-system-arm -M mps2-an500,radio=on -kernel imps2 \e
	-nic socket,model=sx1276,listen=127.0.0.1:11211
qemu-system-arm -M mps2-an500,radio=on -kernel imps2 \e
	-nic socket,model=sx1276,connect=127.0.0.1:11211
.EE
.PP
.B radiochk recv
on the first,
.B "echo -n hello > /dev/radio0/data"
on the second.
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR radio (3)
